  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 247 AM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThe long term period picks up on Tuesday night with Georgia \nunderneath strengthening southwesterly flow aloft ahead of a \nlongwave trough extending from the Upper Midwest towards the Lower \nMississippi River Valley. Advection of deep moisture within this \nflow regime will have dewpoints in the mid 60s and precipitable \nwater values between 1.5-1.8 inches by early Wednesday morning. As a \nsurface low associated with the trough advances across the Great \nLakes, a cold front will extend into the Southeast and move from \nwest to east towards Georgia. Forcing ahead of the front and the \nmoist, unstable airmass will support widespread rain across the \nforecast area. \n\nRain chances are expected to increase across west Georgia during the \nmorning and spread into east Georgia by the evening. Considering the \naforementioned dewpoints and temperatures rising into the mid to \nupper 70s in north Georgia and low 80s in central Georgia, there \nwill be sufficient instability to support scattered thunderstorms. \nWidespread severe weather is not expected at this time with stronger \ndynamics associated with this system likely to be displaced well \nnorth of the forecast area. Rainfall amounts are currently forecast \nto be between 1-2 inches in north Georgia and 0.5-1 inch in central \nGeorgia. The parent low pressure system will become vertically \nstacked on Thursday, leading the front to become occluded before \nentering Georgia. Because of this and building high pressure, \ntemperatures are anticipated to remain a few degrees above normal \neven in the wake of the front in the later parts of the week, with \nhighs in the 70s and overnight lows in the upper 40s to mid 50s.",
